{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1422373,"num_turns":91,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"9a063b41-7f2a-4fcf-9806-479fb0a5ba05","total_cost_usd":8.89758,"usage":{"input_tokens":1275296,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":100844,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":1275296,"outputTokens":100844,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":8.89758,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Cleaned up and verified. Final summary:\n\n## Result\n\n**16/16 workloads pass, bit-exact** (`max_abs = 0.000e+00` — not merely within tolerance), at **~3.3–4.9x** the reference.\n\n## The key finding\n\nProfiling showed this problem has almost no GPU work in it. An **empty** Triton kernel costs ~7.3µs on this machine, and back-to-back null launches drain at that same rate — the GPU sits idle waiting on the host. Meanwhile the largest workload (b=64, s=541) moves 26.6 MB ≈ 3.3µs of HBM time, and the other 15 are under 1µs of real work.\n\nSo the reference's ~35µs was almost entirely dispatch overhead, and the optimization was a host-side latency problem, not a bandwidth one.\n\n## What I did\n\n**Numerics** — the reference's `cat((freqs, freqs), -1)` just duplicates the last axis, so cos/sin of the upper half are identical to the lower half. I compute each value once and store it twice, keeping the exact op order (cos in fp32 → multiply by fp32 scale → single round to bf16). That's why the error is exactly zero rather than merely in-tolerance.\n\n**Host path** (~35µs → ~6µs):\n- Compile once, then call the generated C launcher directly, bypassing `JITFunction.run` — this alone was 7.3µs → 3.5µs\n- Pass raw integer device pointers instead of tensors (−0.4µs)\n- One fused kernel + one allocation for both outputs → single launch, single allocator call\n- Cache all shape-derived values, so a steady-state call is one dict lookup\n- Dropped `@torch.no_grad()` — the function only calls `torch.empty` and a raw launcher, neither of which can record autograd history, so it was 1.5µs of pure overhead\n\n**The one GPU-bound case** — `.wt` (write-through) stores took b=64 from 8.2µs → 6.4µs. Outputs are pure streaming writes nothing re-reads, so allocating L2 lines for them only costs write-allocate traffic. I confirmed bit-identical output before adopting it. Block size 16 was chosen by an in-process A/B across all 16 shapes to cancel machine drift.\n\nI stopped at ~6.4µs on the big case (4.1 TB/s of 8) because five different indexing/store strategies all plateaued there — writes are 2/3 of the traffic, so that appears to be the write-bound limit rather than something my kernel is leaving on the table.\n\n## Verification beyond the harness\n\nI built a local checker against `reference.py` and confirmed bit-exactness across non-contiguous inputs (transposed, strided, last-dim slice), `M` values that exercise the mask path (1, 3, 7, 15, 17, 31, 33), scale values including 0.0 and negatives, extreme magnitudes, true RoPE frequencies, 2-D input, and output-buffer independence. Used 3 of 5 verify attempts.\n\nOne caveat worth stating: the shape cache is keyed on shape alone and stores the device alongside it. That's safe here because exactly one GPU is visible, but it would need a `(shape, device)` key if this were ever run multi-device — I measured that alternative at ~3% slower and chose the faster path given the stated environment.","ttft_ms":8162,"ttft_stream_ms":8135,"time_to_request_ms":76,"type":"result","duration_ms":1547020,"uuid":"b5bf1773-3fb8-49d4-8bd1-6abd5bbe33f8"}
